<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#coartacion-slavery">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coartación</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as coartado</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coartación was a system of self-purchase in the slave societies of Ibero-America from the sixteenth to the nineteenth centuries, by which an enslaved person paid part of the price of freedom before a court and had the rest fixed, becoming a coartado. Growing from custom and slaves' petitions rather than from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siete-partidas">Siete Partidas</a> code, it entered Cuban law only in 1842. A new master could not raise the fixed price, and a coartado gained rights, among them to change masters without showing cause and to a share of the hire when rented out. Women, urban and American-born slaves gained freedom this way most often; it helped create a large population of free people of color in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spanish law, from the Siete Partidas on, left them some rights: to marry in church, to keep savings and to buy their freedom, often in instalments (<b>coartación</b>), and freedom was commoner in towns than in the countryside.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
